The scopes `for_creators` and `for_boards` return `nil` when the filter parameter is not present or is empty. In method chains, calling a method on `nil` would raise a `NoMethodError`. These scopes should return `self` (the relation unchanged) when the filter is not applicable, rather than `nil`. The fix would be: `scope :for_creators, ->(ids) { ids.present? ? where(creator_id: ids) : self }` or similar.
```suggestion
  scope :for_creators, ->(ids) { ids.present? ? where(creator_id: ids) : self }
  scope :for_boards,   ->(ids) { ids.present? ? where(board_id: ids)   : self }
```

Calling `.to_s` on nil values (when nested keys are missing) will produce the string "nil" instead of an empty string. For example, if a `card_board_changed` event lacks the expected nested particulars, the API will return `{"old_board": "nil", "new_board": "nil"}` instead of handling the missing data gracefully. Consider using safe navigation (`&.`) or providing default values.
```suggestion
      { "old_board" => (nested["old_board"] || "").to_s, "new_board" => (nested["new_board"] || "").to_s }
    when "card_title_changed"
      { "old_title" => (nested["old_title"] || "").to_s, "new_title" => (nested["new_title"] || "").to_s }
    when "card_triaged"
      { "column" => (nested["column"] || "").to_s }
```
